WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.300
SPEAKER: If you've
been following along

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.633
with this series, you've got
a pretty good understanding

00:00:03.633 --> 00:00:06.600
of the different techniques that
Cloud DLP uses to find, hide,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.700
and protect sensitive data.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.680
Today, we'll find
out how easy it

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.440
is to build and reuse
your commonly used DLP

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.610
configurations.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.360
Stay tuned.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.180
Kitty Cat Walks has already
been using DLP to inspect

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.540
and de-identify different
data sources like archive chat

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.020
log files, issues from their
ticketing system databases,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.560
and data that they collect
from customers for billing.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.290
Chances are, if you're
anything like Kitty Cat Walks,

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:41.590
the data you want to protect
may be from different sources.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.470
And you may want to
inspect and de-identify

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:48.170
that data consistently using the
same configuration each time.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.630
This is where DLP
templates come in.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.750
Templates allow you to save
DLP configuration details

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.570
and reuse them against any
data that you might have,

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.300
essentially decoupling
configuration information

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.640
from the implementation
of your request.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.810
There are two
types of templates.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.850
An inspection template is
going to save any configuration

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.730
having to do with the process
of inspecting your data,

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.950
like what infotypes
you want to discover,

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.700
a likelihood threshold, maximum
number of findings to return,

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.160
and exclusion rules.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.960
You can even use an
inspection template

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.598
to define your own
custom infotype.

00:01:23.598 --> 00:01:25.640
Let's see what it looks
like to create a template

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.340
with some Node.js code.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.960
Here's an example
of a request that

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.300
will send the
createInspectTemplate function

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.150
to create an inspection template
that's configured to find phone

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.960
numbers and email addresses
with a minimum likelihood

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.408
of possible.

00:01:41.408 --> 00:01:43.700
Once it's been created, here's
what that template looks

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.850
like in our Cloud console.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.340
There's the infotypes
we specified

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.680
and the likelihood too.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.220
Let's edit our
inspection template

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.360
to also include person name.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.020
We go here where it
says Manage Infotypes

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.840
and enter person name
in the search box.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:07.040
Select it and click Done.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.009
Now that we've saved our change,
our inspection template details

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:13.520
page specifies three infotypes.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.740
Note this template path
at the top of the page.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.720
We'll use that in a second.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.730
To see our template in
action, let's check out

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.040
this inspection request.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.740
Instead of constructing an
inspection configuration object

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.875
for this inspection
request, we can simply

00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:28.250
reference the
inspection template

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.035
we created using the
path name like this.

00:02:32.035 --> 00:02:33.410
Our response, of
course, would be

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.360
the results of the inspection.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.150
As expected, we've got a phone
number, an email, and a name.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.490
This is really useful if
you have several inspection

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.500
jobs set up against
different data sources that

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.650
inspect for the same infotypes.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.720
Instead of editing each
inspection configuration

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.440
separately, you can simply
update the template details.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.340
But hold up a second.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.640
What about de-identification?

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.710
Well, the second
type of DLP template

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.700
is a de-identification template.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.380
A de-identification
template is going

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.235
to save any configuration
having to do

00:03:07.235 --> 00:03:10.400
with the process of
de-identifying your data.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.520
For every positive result in
a de-identification request,

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.160
the template will have a
corresponding transformation

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.050
to apply to it.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.550
This is also where
you would specify

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.880
transformation-specific
customizations like replacement

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.470
strings or encryption keys.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.960
De-identification
templates can be

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.330
applied not only to
inspection results,

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:32.480
but also to values in a
specified field in a database

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.310
table.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.110
Here's an example
of how we can use

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.030
the createDeidentifyTemplate
function

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:41.960
to create a new
de-identification template.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.430
Here, we're specifying
that we want

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:48.530
to replace email addresses with
the corresponding infotype.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.690
And we also want to make
sure that the names are

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:54.140
replaced with a custom
replacement value.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:56.403
Once it's been created,
here's what the template looks

00:03:56.403 --> 00:03:57.695
like back in our Cloud console.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.800
Each infotype we specified
and its transformation

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.400
is listed here as a
transformation rule.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.380
Just like our
inspect template, we

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.740
can edit it here to include
additional infotypes.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.880
Let's edit this template to
apply masking to phone numbers.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.060
Down here, we'll add
a transformation rule

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.970
and choose Mask with character.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:26.230
Now we can configure our masking
for this transformation rule.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:29.230
We'll specify the infotype
phone number down here

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.730
where it says Manage Infotypes.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.990
Now that it's saved, there is
our new transformation rule.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.298
De-identification templates
include a test page

00:04:39.298 --> 00:04:41.590
where we can get a quick
preview of the transformations

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.020
we configured in the template.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.290
Emails will be
replaced with infotype,

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:51.070
names are going to be replaced
with a replacement string,

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:54.730
and phone numbers will be
masked with hash symbols.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:56.900
Now let's see our
de-identification template

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.520
do its thing in a
de-identification request.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.830
Instead of including a
deidentifyConfig block

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:06.790
in a request, we can reference
our de-identification template

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:10.425
once again using the path
name from the Details page.

00:05:10.425 --> 00:05:12.550
You'll notice that we're
referencing our inspection

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.230
template from before
to specify what

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:17.110
infotypes we're inspecting for.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:20.370
And here is a response
from the request.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.230
The inspection template
found a phone number, name,

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:24.330
and email address.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.830
And a de-identification template
applied the transformations

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.740
we specified accordingly.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:31.238
Using templates
is a powerful way

00:05:31.238 --> 00:05:33.280
to decouple your inspection
and de-identification

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.080
configuration from
your workflows

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.240
and your implementation.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.920
Inspection templates
can be configured

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:43.030
to inspect up to 150 built-in
and custom infotypes.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:44.590
And de-identification
templates can

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.200
use any combination of
de-identification techniques

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.750
to de-identify the results.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.620
We used a really simple example,
but the value of templates

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.480
really shines for
Kitty Cat Walks when

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.397
they got their data that
needs to be inspected

00:05:57.397 --> 00:06:00.170
and de-identified coming from
their web and mobile apps,

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:02.710
archived on GCS
buckets, and stored

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.690
on a number of databases.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:06.190
Reviewing templates
was a great way

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.170
to bring everything together.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.910
Next time, we'll wrap things up
with an extended episode, where

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:14.320
we'll dive into configuring DLP
in the Google Cloud console.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:14.890
See you soon.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

